 "cards": [
  {
   "dot": "emerald",
   "title": "Source model",
   "items": [
    "Recreation of Fierro, Liccardo and Porcelli (2019), Scientific Reports: spittlebug vectors spreading Xylella fastidiosa through an olive grove",
    "The simulation logic is unchanged; a transmission log records which tree infected which"
   ]
  },
  {
   "dot": "violet",
   "title": "Second route to the same answer",
   "items": [
    "The final infected set of a stochastic SIR epidemic is distributed as a bond-percolation cluster (Grassberger 1983; Newman 2002)",
    "Transmissibility T(d) is measured from one logged run, then outbreak size and the critical planting distance follow without simulating",
    "The predicted critical spacing is confirmed by a full simulation at that spacing"
   ]
  },
  {
   "dot": "slate",
   "title": "What percolation cannot do",
   "items": [
    "Percolation describes the end state and has no clock",
    "The timeline comes from a front moving at constant speed, giving quadratic growth",
    "The annual vector reset turns that curve into a staircase"
   ]
  }
 ]
